0004-69990"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Surgical procedures by body system — the largest section"}]]}],["$","tr","3",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Radiology"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"70010-79999"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Imaging and radiation oncology"}]]}],["$","tr","4",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Pathology and Laboratory"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"80047-89398"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Lab tests and pathology"}]]}],["$","tr","5",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Medicine"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"90281-99607"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Immunizations, cardiovascular studies, therapies"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"CPT Category I sections"}]]}]
1a:["$","div","4",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"Never code directly from the CPT index. The index points you to a code or a range; the main text carries the full description, the parenthetical instructions, and the section guidelines that determine whether the code actually applies. This is the same two-step discipline ICD-10-CM requires, and skipping it produces the same class of error."]}]
1b:["$","div","5",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-6","children":[["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 mb-4","children":"Key terms"}],["$","dl",null,{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"sm:flex sm:gap-4","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 sm:w-48 sm:flex-shrink-0","children":"HCPCS Level II"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"A separate code set — one letter plus four digits — for supplies, durable medical equipment, drugs, prosthetics, and ambulance services. Maintained by CMS."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"sm:flex sm:gap-4","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 sm:w-48 sm:flex-shrink-0","children":"Unlisted procedure code"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"A code used when no specific code describes the service. Requires a written report and is always manually reviewed, so it slows payment."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"sm:flex sm:gap-4","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 sm:w-48 sm:flex-shrink-0","children":"Bundled service"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"A service considered part of another and not separately payable. Reporting it separately is unbundling."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"sm:flex sm:gap-4","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 sm:w-48 sm:flex-shrink-0","children":"Global period"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"A window after a procedure during which related follow-up care is included in the original payment."}]]}]]}]]}]
1c:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"CPT covers physician services and procedures. HCPCS Level II covers what CPT does not — the wheelchair, the injectable drug, the surgical dressing, the ambulance transport. A single encounter can require both: a CPT code for administering an injection and a HCPCS Level II code for the drug that was injected."}]
1d:["$","$L21","7",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"A provider administers a therapeutic injection. Which code or codes are needed?","options":["A CPT code only, since the injection is a physician service","A CPT code for administering the injection and a HCPCS Level II code for the drug","A HCPCS Level II code only, since a drug was supplied","An ICD-10-CM code is sufficient, since it describes the condition treated"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The administration is a service described by CPT; the drug itself is a supply described by HCPCS Level II. Reporting only one leaves the practice unpaid for the other, and this pairing is one of the most common places revenue is lost through incomplete coding.","optionRationales":["CPT covers the administration but has no code for the drug supplied, so the drug goes unbilled.","Correct. CPT for the service, HCPCS Level II for the drug.","HCPCS Level II covers the drug but not the professional work of administering it.","ICD-10-CM explains why care was needed. It never describes what was done and cannot support payment on its own."]}}]
1e:["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-12","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Challenge"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl","children":"Apply what you've learned in this lesson."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-md border border-gray-200 p-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"You will need access to a current CPT code set. Your program or library should provide one; the AMA publishes it annually."}],["$","ol","1",{"start":"$undefined","className":"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 marker:font-semibold marker:text-primary-600","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Locate the six Category I sections in the code book and note the page each begins on. Read the guidelines at the start of the Surgery section."}],["$","li","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Look up a routine office visit in the index, then find the code in the main text. List every piece of information the main text gives you that the index did not."}],["$","li","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Find one Category III code and explain in two sentences why it exists rather than a Category I code."}],["$","li","3",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"For a patient receiving an injectable medication, identify what you would need from the documentation to code both the administration and the drug."}]]}]]}]}]]}]
